WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.160
Hello everyone, welcome back to another episode of the Grand Prixquel Podcast where we discuss

00:04.160 --> 00:10.560
all things feeder series. On today's episode, we are going to be doing a quick look into all

00:10.560 --> 00:17.280
of the F1 Driver Academies. Our hosts today are myself, Robin, Evan, Nova, and Peter.

00:22.080 --> 00:26.800
So, I think we should begin with discussing what essentially is an F1 Driver Academy.

00:27.520 --> 00:33.600
This is how teams can recruit and scout talent and they can essentially help with their pathway

00:33.600 --> 00:39.280
to F1. I mean, it really depends on the academy as to what their support looks like. Some drivers

00:39.280 --> 00:45.360
may give financial support or they may also help with training, providing simulator access, but

00:46.160 --> 00:51.520
it really does vary depending from academy to academy. I know with funding, for example,

00:52.080 --> 00:57.360
Williams helped Logan Sargeant a lot with funding and they essentially helped him move up to F2.

00:57.360 --> 01:03.360
However, with Zak O’Sullivan, he lost funding from his main sponsor and was therefore unable

01:03.360 --> 01:08.480
to complete Formula 2, so it really depends on the driver's personal situation and the team.

01:09.280 --> 01:14.800
I mean, in the case of Zak and Logan, it was the same team, so that even shows how within the same

01:14.800 --> 01:21.600
team they can vary from driver to driver. First off, the driver program I'll talk about is Audi,

01:21.600 --> 01:28.080
which, I mean, if you know me, you know I'm a massive Freddie Slater fan, and Audi's current

01:28.080 --> 01:33.440
program is quite small. I mean, the main driver really is only Freddie, who is, I mean, he really

01:33.440 --> 01:40.160
is destined for Formula 1, I think. He's going to be the Nico Hülkenberg replacement for 2028 and

01:40.560 --> 01:47.440
he's been fast in every level of racing he's ever raced in. I mean, in 2023 he won Ginetta

01:47.440 --> 01:54.080
Juniors, did a few F4 races and took pole in one of them. 2024 absolutely dominated Italian F4,

01:54.080 --> 02:01.680
also winning the Formula 4 UAE Championship. Last year he won FRECA, finished runner-up

02:01.680 --> 02:09.280
in the Middle East, and this year he's currently competing in F3 and finished runner-up in Oceania

02:09.280 --> 02:15.840
in the Formula Regional there. I mean, we're only really one race into F3, so we can't fully

02:15.840 --> 02:21.760
judge, if I'm honest, but he's finished, he's currently P3 in the standings, well tied on points

02:21.760 --> 02:29.200
with P2, I believe. He impressed when he was doing his, like, couple F3 rounds last season.

02:30.000 --> 02:35.280
He did, like, what, two or three rounds, right? He did two rounds, he took a podium on debut,

02:35.280 --> 02:40.800
I mean, he did Bahrain and Spa, Bahrain, he finished P2 in the sprint. He was fighting

02:40.800 --> 02:45.600
for the lead the whole time as well, against Nikola Tsolov, who we'll get onto later,

02:46.320 --> 02:52.880
and even in karting, Freddie is a top talent. He's won multiple karting titles, I mean,

02:52.880 --> 02:58.880
this guy just wins everything he's in. In single seaters, he's never finished below P2 in a

02:58.880 --> 03:04.720
championship, which I think is an insane stat, and I think he's going to continue that on in F3 and

03:04.720 --> 03:09.360
F2, and I mean, he's very much one we're going to see in the F1, like, more of a matter of when

03:09.360 --> 03:14.160
and not if, even though when we pretty much know he's going to do. There are rumours he's already

03:14.160 --> 03:21.520
signed for Invicta for F2 2027, although that's not confirmed, because we know in the F3 Go Trident,

03:21.520 --> 03:26.480
F2 Invicta, they're the best teams at the moment. Does anyone want to add on anything else about

03:26.480 --> 03:32.480
Freddy, or have I, pretty much? I think that's everything. The only other driver who's officially

03:32.480 --> 03:38.880
confirmed to be part of the Audi driver development program is Emma Felbermayr, who does

03:40.240 --> 03:46.560
F1 Academy. She also did the first round of Italian F4, and she scored a point there,

03:47.120 --> 03:52.800
and overall, I've been really impressed by her in F1 Academy this season. I mean,

03:53.360 --> 03:57.680
she's leading the championship, isn't she? Yeah, she is, and I just hope that

03:57.680 --> 04:02.480
Audi can give her some support, and of course, after this year, we can see her move up within

04:02.480 --> 04:06.480
the single-seater world. I hope that her Formula Regional level series, we're going to see

04:06.480 --> 04:11.840
more of her. And I do really hope that Audi continues their support of her, because we see

04:11.840 --> 04:18.240
with a lot of the F1 Academy drivers, because in F1 Academy, each team has a supported driver,

04:18.240 --> 04:23.120
so you often see that a team with a supported driver, but once they leave F1 Academy,

04:23.120 --> 04:26.640
they just get dropped, so I really hope that doesn’t happen to Emma.

04:26.640 --> 04:32.480
Yeah, I really hope they do continue their support with her. In terms of rumors,

04:33.280 --> 04:42.400
we've heard that Daniel Miron, who is a karting driver, should potentially be joining the Academy,

04:43.120 --> 04:49.520
and he's been pretty good. I really do rate him, and I think it would be really cool if Audi do

04:49.520 --> 04:54.320
sign him. Yeah, I guess in the case of Audi that is slowly building up their Academy,

04:54.320 --> 04:59.760
it only got launched, what, two, three months ago or so, so they’re just slowly getting the talent,

04:59.760 --> 05:05.840
and a lot of the time, I think they have also had scouts at FREC. We heard that scouts during the

05:05.840 --> 05:10.480
FEC weekend at the Red Bull Ring, so I think we're bound to see more announcements in the coming

05:10.480 --> 05:16.240
months of who's joining them. And for our next driver academy we have the Ferrari Driver

05:16.240 --> 05:22.080
Academy who I think are quite well known for doing their training camps with their individual drivers,

05:22.080 --> 05:28.480
and they have supported drivers for quite a while. First up on the list would be Rafa

05:28.480 --> 05:36.880
Camara, currently in F2. He's currently P3. Obviously, last year, he was the 2025 F3 champion,

05:36.880 --> 05:42.240
the year before that, he was the 2024 FREC champion. Personally, I don't have much to say

05:42.240 --> 05:48.400
about Kamara, I think he's good, but I do think that sometimes he is carried by the car.

05:48.400 --> 05:56.000
The thing for me with him is like, before the F3 title, he wasn't really talked about that much,

05:56.000 --> 06:02.080
and even the way he won the F3 title, and I mean it was, it was very much take a pole,

06:02.640 --> 06:06.000
do nothing in the feature- in the sprint race, and then win the feature race.

06:06.000 --> 06:11.760
He was in a Trident last year, which we've seen is very dominant in F3, and so

06:11.760 --> 06:16.880
it's hard to kind of gauge how much of it is him and how much of it is the car, to be fair.

06:16.880 --> 06:21.360
And so far in F2, yes, he is P3, but

06:21.360 --> 06:26.880
he's not really moved me that much. Two rounds in, I mean, he's just kind of, if anything,

06:26.880 --> 06:31.520
he's been a bit lucky with misfortunes happening to other drivers, I feel like. I mean,

06:31.520 --> 06:36.560
maybe the Mechachrome will eventually hit him. And even with an F1, I mean, we've seen him

06:36.560 --> 06:42.720
willingly do a test with Ferrari, so maybe later on in the year we'll see a few free practices he

06:42.720 --> 06:48.160
does. We'd hope to see, because if he didn't even do those, I mean, questions need to be asked.

06:48.160 --> 06:51.680
I don't want to delve too deep into rumors,

06:51.680 --> 06:58.960
but there has been a lot of rumors regarding Ferrari and Haas, and if any of those drivers end

06:58.960 --> 07:07.920
up leaving F1, I think Camara should be first in line to take an empty seat for next season.

07:07.920 --> 07:09.840
Oh, definitely, and he definitely has the

07:09.840 --> 07:15.440
Superlicense points to be ready to do it. Yeah, I mean, compared to his F2 counterpart, Dino

07:15.440 --> 07:18.800
Beganovic, I think Camara is definitely the better driver.

07:18.800 --> 07:21.920
And that tends to be the general consensus

07:21.920 --> 07:27.120
when you see him next to Dino, is that a lot of people say that he should be the one getting the

07:27.120 --> 07:37.120
Haas seat, which I do agree with. Dino currently runs P7 in the F2 championship, and he was the 2022

07:37.760 --> 07:41.360
FRECA champion. I mean, with me personally,

07:41.360 --> 07:48.320
I feel like he's not been able to fully maximize since that FRECA title. Okay, 2023, 2024, he's

07:48.320 --> 07:56.480
with Prema in F3, who up until 2024, Prema were the best team in F2, but both of those years he

07:56.480 --> 08:04.480
finished P6. Then last year in F2, I mean, he finished P7, outperformed heavily by Luke Browning.

08:04.480 --> 08:10.800
I mean, this year he has started off strong. He was unlucky to get Mechachromed in Melbourne.

08:10.800 --> 08:16.160
But other than that, I mean, he's one of those guys where he's just kind of there,

08:16.160 --> 08:22.880
I think. And I mean, if you think about since he won FRECA four years ago, it's like you'd expect

08:22.880 --> 08:27.600
to see more career progression, hopefully, if someone is kind of a top level talent.

08:27.600 --> 08:32.960
I think it's really worth mentioning, or last year at least, that Hitech was a bit of an

08:32.960 --> 08:37.120
interesting car to drive. And there was a whole engine problems as well last year with him.

08:37.120 --> 08:41.520
But I don't still don't think that alone means that maybe he is actually a better driver,

08:41.520 --> 08:44.400
because again, Luke Browning also had the same car, exact same car. And I mean,

08:44.400 --> 08:47.680
he finished fourth in the standings. So granted, Luke also had like three other

08:47.680 --> 08:54.080
races the season prior, but... Dino also did a few rounds in 2024,

08:54.080 --> 08:59.440
I believe as well. So they both had experience from the previous year. And he had really good

08:59.440 --> 09:04.160
performances the year before for DAMS, it was, I think. Yeah, it could have honestly just

09:04.160 --> 09:09.360
been the Hitech car last year being bad. But again, if Luke Browning, we'll talk about them

09:09.360 --> 09:15.200
later, nine podiums, one race win. I feel like that's an indication that maybe Dino's not

09:15.200 --> 09:18.640
actually that good, unfortunately, for anyone who thinks he is.

09:18.640 --> 09:22.480
Well, sorry to his fans, if you're listening to this, please don't hate us.

09:23.040 --> 09:29.680
I think next up is we have Tuukka Taponen, who competing in F3, currently P17, but as I mentioned

09:29.680 --> 09:36.320
with Freddie, we're only one round into F3, so we can't fully judge. And I feel like going into

09:36.320 --> 09:42.480
last year into F3, I personally was expecting quite a bit from him, because I feel like, you

09:42.480 --> 09:48.240
know, he'd performed quite well in all of his other previous series. He was P3 in FRECA, he

09:48.240 --> 09:52.240
won the Middle East championship. He won that withR-ace GP, by the way.

09:53.280 --> 09:59.360
But I mean, he only finished P9 in the end, so I mean,  just consistency he struggled with,

09:59.360 --> 10:04.480
because he'd always be like, he'd very much be out of the points a lot, which ultimately is

10:04.480 --> 10:08.880
going to be a downfall, and they seem to be consistently scoring points. If you're out of

10:08.880 --> 10:13.280
the points, that's not going to do anything good for you. So I think for him, he very much just

10:13.280 --> 10:20.480
needs to get a solid F3 season. I feel like I can't rate, really, Taponen. Like, no matter how much

10:20.480 --> 10:27.840
I watch him, I just cannot rate him accordingly. Taponen just kind of is another one that feels

10:27.840 --> 10:33.760
like he's there. Yeah, he's very far. I think he's very much reminded me of Beganovic so far. I was

10:33.760 --> 10:39.600
expecting more, but I feel like he's trending towards that sort of level. It's still good,

10:39.600 --> 10:45.600
but it's like, and he'll have a solid career somewhere, but maybe not quite within F1.

10:46.320 --> 10:52.880
Then next up, we have Alba Larson, who is Ferrari's F1 Academy driver. And I think there's a lot of

10:52.880 --> 10:58.240
expectation on her this year. Like, I think going in, she's very much a driver who F1 Academy hype

10:58.240 --> 11:04.480
up a lot. And I mean, she's very much kind of like a young star, in a way. Young female star.

11:04.480 --> 11:07.040
As much as I really, really like Alba,

11:07.040 --> 11:12.880
I feel like she hasn't really lived up to the expectations. I mean, considering this is her

11:12.880 --> 11:18.160
second season of F1 Academy, obviously she's not a rookie anymore, but she still continues to make

11:18.160 --> 11:23.840
rookie mistakes. And I know she's still young, she's like 17. But she really, if she wants to

11:23.840 --> 11:29.040
do well, she really needs to like polish off those mistakes. I mean, we saw in Shanghai,

11:29.040 --> 11:33.600
for example, you know, with the safety car restart, and she just like ran off track.

11:34.160 --> 11:41.440
And she really, really needs to learn from those mistakes, if she wants, you know, good results,

11:41.440 --> 11:44.720
and if she wants to move up the singles into your ladder.

11:44.720 --> 11:47.760
And the good thing for her is also like,

11:47.760 --> 11:53.440
a lot of the F1 Academy that tracks they've been to last year, like China, they were there last year,

11:53.440 --> 12:00.240
Canada, which they're going to this weekend, she was there last year. So I'd really hope to see her

12:00.240 --> 12:05.920
iron at these rookie errors, because ultimately, they're going to cost her. And if you're struggling

12:05.920 --> 12:10.560
at that F4 level, you can't really progress up to the Formula Regionalbecause it's just going to

12:10.560 --> 12:16.400
be faster cars, the mistakes are going to be more costly. So I just hope she can iron them out. I

12:16.400 --> 12:21.520
mean, we're only one round into F1 Academy. She also should be competing in some

12:21.520 --> 12:24.800
rounds of British F4 this year. I'd imagine they could be the

12:24.800 --> 12:30.400
Silverstone and Zandvoort round, because of course, there's a track to F1 Academy also where she's on.

12:30.400 --> 12:35.360
I was a bit disappointed to not see her do somewhere like Brands Hatch and Donington Park,

12:35.360 --> 12:40.000
given they didn't clash with F1 Academy at all. Yeah, I would have really liked to see

12:40.000 --> 12:44.240
her do like almost a full campaign, minus obviously the stuff that clashes.

12:44.240 --> 12:47.840
Aside from her, we have Niccolo

12:47.840 --> 12:55.120
Maccagnani, F4 driver again competing in Italy. Currently, I think he's outperformed what I've

12:55.120 --> 12:58.480
expected of him so far. I think we've talked about this

12:58.480 --> 13:06.400
really recently with the Italian F4 recap episode, but I feel like he really has exceeded my

13:06.400 --> 13:12.800
expectations. I did not expect him to do so well, because during UAE4, he was kind of a bit of a

13:12.880 --> 13:18.880
reverse grid merchant. So obviously Italian F4 doesn't have reverse grids, so I wasn't really

13:18.880 --> 13:23.280
expecting him to do very well, but he's done surprisingly well.

13:23.280 --> 13:25.600
And I can say this for so many other of these

13:25.600 --> 13:30.160
championships. They're only one round earned, so a lot of it is yet to be seen.

13:30.160 --> 13:33.840
I'm pleasantly surprised by him, very pleasantly

13:33.840 --> 13:39.360
surprised. I was a bit more scared for that lineup, but he's definitely pulling his weight.

13:40.000 --> 13:42.400
Yeah, so I think as for the karting drivers,

13:42.400 --> 13:48.320
I think Noah Baglin is a very notable name. He currently competes in OK Senior with Cart Republic,

13:48.320 --> 13:56.160
who are basically Prema and he leads the FIA European Championship. He won titles, he won the

13:56.160 --> 14:03.920
OKJ World Championships last year and Champions of the Future. He won WSK in 2024, and he plans to

14:03.920 --> 14:12.320
debut in F4 next year. Unfortunately, because he turns 15 in March, end of March next year,

14:12.320 --> 14:18.640
we're not going to see him do any winter series. He has so much going to be with one main F4

14:18.640 --> 14:23.200
campaign from him next year, no winter series, but it's still going to be fun to see.

14:23.200 --> 14:26.880
I made a joke about Noah Baglin doing

14:26.880 --> 14:34.160
USF Juniors because he's 14 and the minimum age for USF Juniors is 14. So I said,

14:34.160 --> 14:41.120
oh, Noah Baglan should do USF Juniors as a joke. But I think Dean Hoogendoorn took that seriously,

14:41.120 --> 14:45.920
and we'll get into that later because Dean Hoogerndoorn is also part of a driver academy.

14:45.920 --> 14:50.640
But I thought it was really funny that a driver actually just went from Europe,

14:50.640 --> 14:55.440
Karting in Europe, to do USF Juniors, and he's doing the whole season.

14:55.440 --> 14:59.920
They have Felipe Sala, who's another karting driver,

14:59.920 --> 15:05.920
but there's nothing too notable about him, but he's solid. Again, we know with karting,

15:05.920 --> 15:12.080
we need to very much see if they're able to translate into cars because

15:12.720 --> 15:15.520
karting talent doesn't always equal car racing talent.

15:16.160 --> 15:21.360
He does turn 15 soon, in July, so we could

15:21.360 --> 15:27.280
potentially see him in maybe for a couple of rounds at the end of Italian F4, or we'll see

15:27.280 --> 15:33.680
him over the winter. He does have that chance that Noah Baglin doesn't have because he's not old

15:33.680 --> 15:36.320
enough. Yeah, I think. And the FDA have

15:36.320 --> 15:41.920
actually sent quite a few drivers to F1, notably Jules Bianchi, Sergio Perez,

15:42.000 --> 15:47.440
Charles Leclerc, Mick Schumacher, and Olly Bearman. There's definitely a path for the FDA

15:47.440 --> 15:54.000
drivers to get to F1, and as we mentioned earlier, there is links with Haas, we’ve seen with Bearman

15:55.520 --> 16:00.080
Bearman and Schumacher both were at Haas, Leclerc started at Sauber.

16:00.080 --> 16:04.320
And also a lot of the FDA members have also

16:04.320 --> 16:09.520
gone through to IndyCar, which I think is certainly something.

16:10.160 --> 16:11.840
Well, that's Shwartzman,

16:13.280 --> 16:14.560
Ilott, who else?

16:14.560 --> 16:15.920
Armstrong.

16:15.920 --> 16:17.600
Was Armstrong as well, yeah.

16:17.600 --> 16:20.080
I mean, you have Schumacher as well now.

16:20.080 --> 16:24.320
And in IndyNXT, we have Enzo Fittipaldi as well.

16:24.320 --> 16:31.040
FDA is an IndyCar driver academy, not an F1 driver academy. You heard it here first.

16:31.040 --> 16:35.760
We announced so much news on the Grand Prixquel

16:35.760 --> 16:39.040
podcast. So much official news.

16:39.040 --> 16:43.040
So you basically go FDA to IndyCar,

16:43.040 --> 16:45.440
I mean, IndyCar is a feeder series to EC3.

16:45.440 --> 16:47.840
What does EC3 feed it to?

16:47.840 --> 16:52.080
Anyways, I think another notable junior team,

16:52.080 --> 16:56.720
very much like Ferrari who are known for their training in a way, is the Red Bull Junior Team.

16:56.720 --> 17:02.000
You see a lot of them on TikTok, on Instagram, you see the training they do with their drivers,

17:02.400 --> 17:06.640
obviously do a lot helping their drivers with their physical strength, and their current reaction

17:06.640 --> 17:12.000
times, that sort of thing. I think one of their most notable drivers is Nikola Tsolov,

17:12.000 --> 17:19.200
currently leads the F2 Championship, and he's a 2022 Spanish F4 Champion. I mean, he is kind

17:19.200 --> 17:22.480
of a meme, but at the same time, he's proving himself in F2.

17:22.480 --> 17:26.000
Again, not to feed into rumors too much,

17:26.080 --> 17:35.200
but he is very, very heavily rumored to be in a VCARB seat next year, whatever happens. I

17:35.200 --> 17:40.080
obviously don't know what's going to happen, whether you have Max leaving the sport, or if

17:40.080 --> 17:48.960
they decide to drop Liam, or if Isack gets dropped, who knows. But whatever happens,

17:49.840 --> 17:52.640
Tsolov is supposed to be the next in line.

17:53.280 --> 17:58.320
I mean, I think in F3, he didn't fully live up to expectations, but I think part of that

17:58.320 --> 18:04.080
was just him skipping Formula Regional, which of course he went to Spanish F4 right into F3,

18:04.080 --> 18:09.840
